
======================================================================================================
DOES THE 1-LOOP dS EDGE SURVIVE THE alpha=2 KERNEL? -- structural inputs, derived from scratch
======================================================================================================
  a0 = c H_Lambda/Z, Z = 5.78881 -> 9.3600e-11 m/s^2 canonical; alt 1.1300e-10.
  kappa = 1/2 is Carl's and stays FITTED, not derived.
  SCOPE, STATED BEFORE THE RESULT: this file does NOT re-run the full 1-loop divergence
  computation. It establishes whether every structural input the alpha=1 argument used holds on
  the alpha=2 measure, and finds where the two can differ. The conclusion is CONDITIONAL on the
  published proof having used only those inputs -- a proviso repeated in the verdict.

======================================================================================================
S1. DERIVE the Herglotz-Nevanlinna representation of K_2 -- not assume it
======================================================================================================
  Claim:  K_2(z) = sqrt(z/(1+z)) = 1 - Integral_0^1 rho(s) ds/(z+s),  rho = (1/pi)sqrt(s/(1-s)).
  Substitute s = sin^2(theta), ds = 2 sin cos dtheta, sqrt(s/(1-s)) = tan(theta):
      I(z) = (2/pi) Integral_0^{pi/2} sin^2 t /(z + sin^2 t) dt
           = (2/pi)[ pi/2 - z Integral_0^{pi/2} dt/(z + sin^2 t) ]
  so everything reduces to one standard integral. Verify THAT symbolically:
  Verify that standard integral at 50 digits over a spread of (a,b) -- sympy can do it but
  grinds for minutes on the symbolic form, and a 50-digit check over many points is no weaker:
           a        b               quadrature      pi/(2 sqrt(a(a+b)))     |diff|
        0.01        1        15.63000763406166        15.63000763406166        0.0
         0.5        1        1.813799364234218        1.813799364234218        0.0
           1        1        1.110720734539592        1.110720734539592   2.67e-51
           3        1       0.4534498410585545       0.4534498410585545        0.0
         100        1      0.01563000763406166      0.01563000763406166        0.0
           1      0.5        1.282549830161864        1.282549830161864        0.0
           1        7       0.5553603672697958       0.5553603672697958   1.34e-51
         1e4        1    0.0001570717792868552    0.0001570717792868552        0.0
  [OK  ] the standard integral equals pi/(2 sqrt(a(a+b))) to 2.67e-51 across eight (a,b) pairs at 50 digits, so with a=z, b=1 it is pi/(2 sqrt(z(z+1)))

    assembling: I(z) = 1 - (2z/pi)*pi/(2 sqrt(z(z+1))) = -sqrt(z)/sqrt(z + 1) + 1
    then 1 - I(z) = sqrt(z)/sqrt(z + 1)   vs  K_2(z) = sqrt(z)/sqrt(z + 1)
  [OK  ] *** 1 - I(z) = K_2(z) IDENTICALLY (residual 0) -- the representation is DERIVED, with positive density rho = (1/pi)sqrt(s/(1-s)) on the compact support (0,1) ***

  INDEPENDENT NUMERICAL CHECK of the original s-integral at 50 digits:
             z       1 - Int rho/(z+s) ds                 K_2(z)     |diff|
          1e-4     0.00999950003749687527 0.00999950003749687527   4.55e-28
          1e-2      0.0995037190209989136  0.0995037190209989136    4.5e-28
           0.5       0.577350269189625765   0.577350269189625765   3.03e-28
             1       0.707106781186547524   0.707106781186547524   2.27e-28
             3       0.866025403784438647   0.866025403784438647   1.01e-29
           1e2       0.995037190209989136   0.995037190209989136   3.98e-31
           1e4       0.999950003749687527   0.999950003749687527   4.02e-33
           1e6          0.999999500000375      0.999999500000375   4.02e-35
  [OK  ] the representation holds numerically to 4.55e-28 across eight decades -- the symbolic derivation and the direct quadrature agree
  [OK  ] MUTATION: perturbing rho by a factor (1+s/10) breaks the representation at z=1 by 0.0207 -- so the check discriminates the measure rather than passing for any positive density

======================================================================================================
S2. *** THE SUM RULE IS AN IDENTITY -- so the a0-protection mechanism is MEASURE-INDEPENDENT ***
======================================================================================================
  The alpha=1 1-loop argument used a sum rule quoted as  Int dmu/|t| = 1.
  For a Herglotz kernel written K(z) = K(inf) - Integral rho(s) ds/(z+s), set z -> 0 and z -> inf:
      K(inf) = K(inf)                     (the integral vanishes as z -> inf)
      K(0)   = K(inf) - Integral rho/s ds
  hence  Integral rho/s ds = K(inf) - K(0)   IDENTICALLY, for ANY such kernel.
  So the sum rule equalling 1 is not a property of a particular measure -- it is exactly the
  pair of boundary conditions  K(inf) = 1  and  K(0) = 0.

    alpha=2:  Integral rho ds   = 1/2      (total mass)
    alpha=2:  Integral rho/s ds = 1      (the sum rule)
  [OK  ] alpha=2 satisfies the sum rule EXACTLY: Integral rho/s ds = 1 = 1, in closed form, not numerically
  [OK  ] and its total mass is exactly 1/2 -- finite, unlike alpha=1 (see S3)

  BOUNDARY VALUES OF BOTH KERNELS (mpmath, 50 dps):
      kernel                 K(1e-30)                  K(1e30)
     alpha=1                  1.0e-15                      1.0
     alpha=2                  1.0e-15                      1.0
  [OK  ] *** BOTH kernels satisfy K(0) = 0 (horizon floor) and K(inf) = 1 (passivity saturation) *** -- so both satisfy the sum rule, and the mechanism that protected a0 at 1 loop on alpha=1 is present unchanged on alpha=2. It was never a feature of the alpha=1 measure

  READ THIS CAREFULLY, because it is the result: K(0) = 0 says the kernel has NO zero-frequency
  piece. A 1-loop divergence that cannot generate a DC term cannot shift a0, which is the scale
  in K(Box_u/a0^2). The protection is structural -- it follows from the horizon floor and
  passivity, both of which are premises of the construction rather than accidents of a fit.
  [OK  ] MUTATION: adding a constant 0.3 to the kernel gives K(0) = 0.3 != 0, which breaks the sum rule to 1 - 0.3 = 0.7 and would leave a DC term free to shift a0 -- so K(0) = 0 is doing real work and is not a vacuous condition

======================================================================================================
S3. MOMENTS: alpha=2 is STRICTLY BETTER BEHAVED -- alpha=1's measure has divergent mass
======================================================================================================
  Any 1-loop quantity whose coefficient involves Integral dmu, or a moment of it, needs those
  integrals to converge. Compute the alpha=2 moments in closed form:
      n          Integral rho s^n ds        decimal
      0            beta(3/2, 1/2)/pi     0.50000000
      1            beta(5/2, 1/2)/pi     0.37500000
      2            beta(7/2, 1/2)/pi     0.31250000
      3            beta(9/2, 1/2)/pi     0.27343750
      4           beta(11/2, 1/2)/pi     0.24609375
  [OK  ] all five leading moments are FINITE, POSITIVE and closed-form rational multiples of unity (beta(3/2, 1/2)/pi, beta(5/2, 1/2)/pi, beta(7/2, 1/2)/pi, beta(9/2, 1/2)/pi, beta(11/2, 1/2)/pi) -- the compact support (0,1) guarantees every moment exists

  NOW THE COMPARISON THAT MATTERS. The alpha=1 kernel behaves as K_1(z) -> sqrt(z) for small z
  and its spectral support is UNBOUNDED, giving a DIVERGENT total mass. Demonstrate the
  divergence directly from the kernel's own large-z behaviour rather than asserting it:
           z       z*(1 - K_1(z))       z*(1 - K_2(z))
         1e2          4.875078027          0.496280979
         1e4          49.87500078         0.4999625031
         1e6              499.875          0.499999625
         1e8             4999.875         0.4999999963
        1e10            49999.875                  0.5
  [OK  ] z(1-K) GROWS without bound for alpha=1 (4.87508 -> 49999.9) but SETTLES to a constant for alpha=2 (0.496281 -> 0.5 ~ 1/2, the total mass). So alpha=1's measure has divergent mass and alpha=2's does not

  CONSEQUENCE, and it is an improvement rather than merely a change: any 1-loop coefficient
  requiring Integral dmu was, on alpha=1, a divergent integral needing regularization. On
  alpha=2 it is finite (1/2) with no regulator. Whatever the alpha=1 argument had to handle by
  hand there, alpha=2 hands over for free.

======================================================================================================
S4. PASSIVITY ON THE LOOP DOMAIN -- and the ONE place alpha=2 is WORSE than alpha=1
======================================================================================================
  Kallen-Lehmann positivity needs rho >= 0; passivity needs |K| <= 1 wherever the loop samples.
             z             K_1(z)             K_2(z)   rho_2 at s=z (if <1)
          1e-8       9.9999999e-5      9.99999995e-5           3.1830989e-5
          1e-4   0.00999900019995    0.0099995000375            0.003183258
          1e-2    0.0990195135928     0.099503719021            0.031991347
           0.5     0.517638090205      0.57735026919             0.31830989
             1      0.61803398875     0.707106781187                      -
            10     0.854308953557     0.953462589246                      -
           1e4     0.995012499922      0.99995000375                      -
           1e8      0.99995000125        0.999999995                      -
          1e16        0.999999995                1.0                      -
  [OK  ] 0 <= K_2(z) <= 1 across sixteen decades of z > 0, so passivity holds on the Euclidean loop domain, where the integration actually lives
  [OK  ] rho_2(s) >= 0 at all 999 sampled interior points (0 negatives) -- manifestly so, since sqrt(s/(1-s))/pi is a positive function on (0,1). Kallen-Lehmann positivity is therefore automatic and needs no dressing argument

  *** AND THE HONEST DEBIT, which runs against the alpha=2 switch: ***
             z     K_2(z) = sqrt(z/(1+z))
         -1.01              10.0498756211
          -1.1              3.31662479036
            -2              1.41421356237
           -10              1.05409255339
  [OK  ] for z < -1 the alpha=2 kernel EXCEEDS unity (K_2(-1.01) = 10.049876 > 1), violating passivity there -- a defect alpha=1 does not share in the same place, since its branch point sits at z = -1/4
  This does NOT invalidate the loop result, because the Euclidean loop domain is z > 0 where
  S4's first check holds. But it IS a condition on the contour, not a theorem: any calculation
  that analytically continues past z = -1 must re-examine positivity there rather than inherit
  it. That is the single identified place where the alpha=2 loop edge could differ from alpha=1,
  and it is flagged rather than buried.

======================================================================================================
S5. THE LINEAR VERTEX IS KERNEL-INDEPENDENT -- the geodesy theorem transfers with zero input
======================================================================================================
  The v11 result 'linear vertex zero to all orders (geodesy theorem)' is the one lane that
  needs no measure at all, and it is worth showing why rather than assuming it carries over.
  The u-contracted first moment is <Box_u>_u = +|a|^2 exactly (from u.u = -1). Whatever K is,
  the coupling is a function of that scalar, so the linear-in-perturbation vertex is
      delta[ F(|a|^2) ] = F'(|a|^2) * delta(|a|^2) = F'(|a|^2) * 2 a . delta a,
  which vanishes identically when the background acceleration a = 0, i.e. on a geodesic.

    d/d(eps) F(|a + eps*da|^2) at eps=0 = 2*(a1*d1 + a2*d2 + a3*d3)*Subs(Derivative(F(_xi_1), _xi_1), _xi_1, a1**2 + a2**2 + a3**2)
  [OK  ] the linear vertex vanishes identically on a geodesic background (a = 0), for a COMPLETELY GENERIC F -- sympy gives 0. No property of K, and no property of the measure, is used. The geodesy theorem therefore transfers to alpha=2 with nothing to check
  [OK  ] MUTATION: off a geodesic (a = (1,0,0), da = (1,0,0)) the vertex is 2*Subs(Derivative(F(_xi_1), _xi_1), _xi_1, 1) != 0, so the vanishing is a genuine consequence of a = 0 and not an artefact of the differentiation

======================================================================================================
VERDICT
======================================================================================================
  THE STRUCTURAL INPUTS ALL TRANSFER, AND THE CENTRAL ONE TRANSFERS FOR A REASON RATHER THAN BY
  LUCK -- which is the actual result here:

   1. THE SUM RULE IS AN IDENTITY. For any Herglotz kernel, Integral rho/s ds = K(inf) - K(0).
      So the alpha=1 argument's 'Int dmu/|t| = 1' was never a property of that measure: it is
      exactly K(inf) = 1 (passivity saturation) and K(0) = 0 (horizon floor). alpha=2 satisfies
      both -- Int rho/s ds = 1 in CLOSED FORM -- so the mechanism protecting a0 is intact.
      And the physics is now visible: K(0) = 0 means the kernel carries no zero-frequency piece,
      and a divergence that cannot generate a DC term cannot shift the scale inside
      K(Box_u/a0^2). The protection follows from two PREMISES of the construction, not a fit.

   2. THE REPRESENTATION IS DERIVED, not quoted: K_2 = 1 - Int_0^1 rho/(z+s) ds with
      rho = (1/pi)sqrt(s/(1-s)), via s = sin^2(theta) and one standard integral, checked against
      direct quadrature to better than 1e-20 over eight decades, with a mutation control.

   3. alpha=2 IS STRICTLY BETTER BEHAVED. Its measure has compact support (0,1), total mass
      exactly 1/2, and all moments finite in closed form. alpha=1's mass DIVERGES -- shown
      directly from z(1-K) growing without bound. Any 1-loop coefficient needing Int dmu was a
      divergent integral on alpha=1 and is finite with no regulator on alpha=2.

   4. KALLEN-LEHMANN POSITIVITY IS AUTOMATIC: rho_2 >= 0 manifestly on its whole support, so it
      needs no dressing argument at all.

   5. THE LINEAR VERTEX IS KERNEL-FREE. delta[F(|a|^2)] = 2 F' a . delta a vanishes on a
      geodesic for GENERIC F, so the geodesy theorem transfers with nothing to verify.

  *** THE ONE PLACE alpha=2 IS WORSE, AND IT IS NOT SWEPT UP: *** K_2 EXCEEDS UNITY for z < -1
  (K_2(-1.01) = 10.05), violating passivity there, where alpha=1's branch point at z = -1/4
  puts its own trouble elsewhere. The Euclidean loop domain is z > 0, where 0 <= K_2 <= 1 over
  sixteen decades, so the loop result is not invalidated -- but this is a CONDITION ON THE
  CONTOUR, not a theorem. Any calculation continuing past z = -1 must re-examine positivity
  rather than inherit it.

  WHAT IS THEREFORE ESTABLISHED, precisely: the 1-loop conclusion -- a0 unrenormalized, linear
  vertex zero, KL positivity and KMS preserved -- TRANSFERS to the kernel in force, PROVIDED the
  published proof used only the inputs verified above. That proviso is real and I am not
  pretending otherwise: I have not re-run the divergence computation, and a step that reached
  past these inputs into the detailed shape of the alpha=1 measure would need redoing.

  STILL OPEN, and named rather than glossed: two loops; the finite parts; the T_mu_nu metric
  variation; the disformal rho_m variant; the ephemeris de/dt bound. None is touched here.
  a0 is NOT derived, kappa = 1/2 stays FITTED, the pincer is untouched (Theorem 3 forbids all
  local L, Theorem 8's argument mismatch stands), and no door is declared closed.
======================================================================================================
